====Adjective====
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|usually|not comparable}} Physically [[unobstructed]], [[uncovered]], etc.
## Able to have something [[pass]] through or along it.
##: {{ux|en|Come in – the door's '''open'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|The ice has cleared and the channel is '''open''' again.}}
## Not [[covered]], [[sealed]], etc.; having an [[opening]] or [[aperture]] showing what is [[inside]]. 
##: {{ux|en|The jewellery box was lying '''open'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|Don't just stand there with your mouth '''open'''!}}
##: {{ux|en|Along the street ran an '''open''' sewer.}}
## Not physically [[drawn]] [[together]], [[folded]] or [[contracted]].
##: {{ux|en|She greeted them with '''open''' arms.}}
##: {{ux|en|The book was '''open''' at page 23.}}
##: {{ux|en|an '''open''' hand; an '''open''' flower; an '''open''' (loosely woven) fabric}}
##* {{RQ:Dryden Fables|The Flower and the Leaf
|passage=Each, with '''open''' arms, embraced her chosen knight.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2005|author=Pamela J. Carter; Susan Lewsen|title=Lippincott's Textbook for Nursing Assistants|page=277
|passage=When the top sheet, blanket, and bedspread of a closed bed are turned back, or fanfolded, the closed bed becomes an '''open''' bed, or a bed ready to receive a patient or resident.}}
## Of a space, free of objects and obstructions.
##: {{ux|en|It was a large, '''open''' room.}}
##: {{ux|en|Most of the site was occupied by huts, but there was an '''open''' area in the centre.}}
##: {{ux|en|Soon we left the forest behind and we were out in '''open''' country.}}
##* {{RQ:Grahame Wind in the Willows|passage=The '''open''' road, the dusty highway{{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|medicine}} Resulting from an incision, puncture or any other process by which the skin no longer protects an internal part of the body.
##: {{ux|en|an '''open''' fracture}}
## {{lb|en|engineering|gas and liquid flow|of valve or damper}} In a position allowing fluid to flow.
## {{lb|en|of a sandwich, etc.|usually|not comparable}} Composed of a single [[slice]] of [[bread]] with a [[topping]].
##: {{syn|en|open-face|open-faced}}
##: {{co|en|[['''open''' sandwich]]}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Jennie Walters|title=Caz’s Birthday Blues|series=Party Girls|url=https://archive.org/details/cazsbirthdayblue0000walt/page/107/mode/1up|location=London|publisher=[[w:Hodder & Stoughton|Hodder Children’s Books]]|year=2001|isbn=0 340 79586 7|passage=Starry food is fun to make. You can buy bright yellow American mustard (which isn’t too strong!) in squeezy bottles and pipe stars on to hot dogs and '''open''' burgers or sandwiches.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Jo McAuley|chapter=[Meat and Poultry] Turkey Burgers with Spicy Salsa|title=Hamlyn QuickCook: Low Fat|location=London|publisher=[[w:Hamlyn (publisher)|Hamlyn]]|year=2012|page=152|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/lowfat0000mcau/page/152/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-60062-368-7|passage=When the burgers are ready, place them on the toasted rolls with the romaine lettuce leaves and top with the salsa. Serve as '''open''' burgers.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Michael Robotham|chapter=17|title=Close Your Eyes|location=London|publisher=[[w:Sphere Books|Sphere]]|year=2015|page=133|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/closeyoureyes0000robo_i8j7/page/133/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-7515-5287-4|passage=Sunday morning in Wellow and we feast on '''open''' bagels with grilled ham, tomato and Swiss cheese, requested and highly praised.}}
## {{lb|en|now|regional}} Not of a quality to prevent communication, as by closing waterways, blocking roads, etc.; hence, not frosty or inclement; mild; used of the weather or the climate.
##: {{ux|en|an '''open''' winter}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1794|author=w:Jane Austen|title=Lady Susan
|passage=He desires me to tell you that the present '''open''' weather induces him to accept Mr Vernon's invitation to prolong his stay in Sussex that they may have some hunting together.}}
# Able to be used or interacted with in some way.
## Available for use or participation.
##: {{ux|en|Your bank account is now '''open'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|Phone lines '''open''' at 10 pm.}}
##: {{ux|en|This is the only option '''open''' to us.}}
## {{lb|en|not comparable}} Actively conducting or prepared to [[conduct]] [[business]].
##: {{ux|en|Banks are not '''open''' on bank holidays.}}
## Allowing entrance to visitors or the public.
##: {{ux|en|I hereby declare this fete '''open'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|The school has an '''open''' day on Saturday.}}
## {{lb|en|comparable}} [[receptive|Receptive]].
##: {{ux|en|I am '''open''' to new ideas.}}
##* {{RQ:King James Version|book=Acts|chapter=19|verse=38|passage=Wherefore if Demetrius {{...}} have a matter against any man, the law is '''open''' and there are deputies.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Time|author=Ciara Nugent|title=Can Public Transit Survive the Pandemic? London's New Transport Commissioner Wants You to Believe It Can|date=April 2 2021
|passage=A U.K. survey found attitudes toward public transit had been set back by two decades, with only 43% of drivers '''open''' to using their car less, even if public transport improves.
|url=https://time.com/5951769/andy-byford-mta-tfl-interview/}}
## {{lb|en|comparable|with 'to'}} Susceptible or vulnerable (to the stated means).
##: {{ux|en|'''open''' to question; '''open''' to attack; '''open''' to criticism}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2|act=V|scene=ii|passage=The service that I truly did his life, / Hath left me '''open''' to all injuries.}}
## {{lb|en|computing|not comparable|of a file, document, etc.}} In current use; connected to as a resource.
##: {{ux|en|I couldn't save my changes because another user had the same file '''open'''.}}
# Not hidden or restricted.
## Not concealed; [[overt]].
##: {{ux|en|It is a blatant example of '''open''' criminality.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merry Wives|act=I|scene=iii|passage=His thefts were too '''open'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=III|passage=That I may find him, and with secret gaze / Or '''open''' admiration him behold.|year=1873}}
## Of a person, not concealing their feelings, opinions, etc.; [[candid]], [[ingenuous]].
##: {{ux|en|Nowadays people are more '''open''' about their sexuality.}}
##* '''1731-1735''', {{w|Alexander Pope}}, ''Moral Essays''
##*: with aspect '''open''', shall erect his head
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|I|iii|passage=The Moor is of a free and '''open''' nature.}}
##* {{RQ:Addison Italy
|passage=The French are always '''open''', familiar, and talkative.}}
##* {{quote-book
|en
|year=2001
|author=Xiaopei He
|chapter=Chinese Queer (''Tongzhi'') Women Organizing in the 1990s
|editors=Ping-Chun Hsiung; Maria Jaschok; Cecilia Milwertz; Red Chan
|title=Chinese Women Organizing: Cadres, Feminists, Muslims, Queers
|url=https://archive.org/details/chinesewomenorga0000unse/
