|LCCN=2001005195
|OCLC=1171258066
|page=41
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/chinesewomenorga0000unse/page/41/
|text=Due to severe and pervasive discrimination, people dared not be '''open''' about their homosexuality, and because no one would be '''open''', social prejudice and discrimination became even stronger.}}
## {{senseid|en|public}}{{lb|en|not comparable}} [[public|Public]].
##: {{ux|en|He published an '''open''' letter to the governor on a full page of ''The New York Times''.}}
## {{lb|en|law|of [[correspondence]]}} Written or sent with the intention that it may made public or referred to at any [[trial]], rather than by way of [[confidential]] private [[negotiation]] for a [[settlement]].
##: {{ux|en|You will observe that this is an '''open''' letter and we reserve the right to mention it to the judge should the matter come to trial.}}
## {{senseid|en|of open access and open science}} {{lb|en|not comparable}} With [[open access]], of {{w|open science}}, or both.
##: {{ux|en|We hope that all aspects of the project will be '''open''' rather than paywalled.}}
## {{lb|en|computing|education}} Made public, usable with a free licence and without [[proprietary]] components.
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-07-20|volume=408|issue=8845|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/http://www.economist.com/news/business/21582001-army-new-online-courses-scaring-wits-out-traditional-universities-can-they The attack of the MOOCs]
|passage=Since the launch early last year of&nbsp;[&hellip;] two Silicon Valley start-ups offering free education through MOOCs, massive '''open''' online courses, the ivory towers of academia have been shaken to their foundations. University brands built in some cases over centuries have been forced to contemplate the possibility that information technology will rapidly make their existing business model obsolete.}}
## {{lb|en|sports}} Of a tournament or competition, allowing anyone to enter, especially or originally irrespective of professional or amateur status.
# Not completed or finalised.
## Not settled; not decided or determined; not withdrawn from consideration.
##: {{ux|en|an '''open''' question}}
##: {{ux|en|to keep an offer or opportunity '''open'''}}
## {{lb|en|sometimes|business}} Not fulfilled or resolved; incomplete.
##: {{ux|en|I've got '''open''' orders for as many containers of red durum as you can get me.}}
## Not having one end joined to the other; not forming a closed loop.
##: {{ux|en|an '''open''' curve, an '''open''' circuit}}
## {{lb|en|electricity|of a switch or circuit breaker}} In a position such that a circuit is not completed, preventing electricity from flowing.
## {{lb|en|graph theory|of a [[walk]]}} Whose first and last vertices are different.
## {{lb|en|phonetics|of a syllable}} That ends in a [[vowel]]; not having a [[coda]].
# {{lb|en|mathematics|logic|of a [[formula]]}} Having a [[free]] [[variable]].
# {{lb|en|mathematics|topology|of a [[set]]}} Which is part of a predefined collection of subsets of <math>X</math>, that defines a [[topological space]] on <math>X</math>.
# {{lb|en|music|stringed instruments}} Of a note, played without pressing the string against the [[fingerboard]].
# {{lb|en|wind instruments}} Of a note, played without closing any finger-hole, key or valve.
# {{lb|en|phonetics}} Uttered with a relatively wide opening of the articulating organs; said of [[vowel]]s.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1959|year_published=1972|author=w:Anthony Burgess|title=Beds in the East|series=The Malayan Trilogy|page=421|passage="Supposing somebody sees you, with all those flowers too? Supposing somebody writes him a letter? Ooooh!" (a pure round '''open''' Tamil O.)}}
# {{lb|en|phonetics}} Uttered, as a [[consonant]], with the oral passage simply narrowed without closure.
# {{lb|en|computing|used before "code"}} [[source code|Source code]] of a computer program that is not within the text of a [[macro]] being generated.
# {{lb|en|of a multi-word compound}} Having component words separated by spaces, as opposed to being joined together or hyphenated; for example, ''time slot'' as opposed to ''timeslot'' or ''time-slot''.
# {{lb|en|sports|and|games}} Characterised by free-flowing play.
#: {{ux|en|Compared to their last match, which was a dour and defensive affair, this was a very '''open''' game.}}

