===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|openen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|openian|t=to open}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*opanōn}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*upanōną|t=to raise; lift; open}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*upanaz|t=open|pos=adjective}}.

Cognate with {{cog|stq|eepenje|t=to open}}, {{cog|fy|iepenje|t=to open}}, {{cog|nl|openen|t=to open}}, {{cog|de|öffnen|t=to open}}, {{cog|da|åbne|t=to open}}, {{cog|sv|öppna|t=to open}}, {{cog|nb|åpne|t=to open}}, {{cog|nn|-}} and {{cog|is|opna|t=to open}}. Related to {{cog|en|up}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}
# To make or become physically unobstructed, uncovered, etc.
## {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To make or become accessible or clear for passage by moving from a [[shut]] position.
##: {{ux|en|Turn the doorknob to '''open''' the door.}} 
##: {{ux|en|The door '''opened''' all by itself.}}
##* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=7|passage=I made a speaking trumpet of my hands and commenced to whoop “Ahoy!” and “Hello!” at the top of my lungs. {{...}} The Colonel woke up, and, after asking what in brimstone was the matter, '''opened''' his mouth and roared “Hi!” and “Hello!” like the bull of Bashan.}}
##* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=20|passage=&lsquo;No. I only '''opened''' the door a foot and put my head in. The street lamps shine into that room. I could see him. He was all right. Sleeping like a great grampus. Poor, poor chap.’}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To make or become clear by removal of objects and obstructions, so as to allow passage, [[access]], or [[visibility]].
##: {{ux|en|The icebreaker '''opened''' the channel.}}
##: {{ux|en|The ice melted away and the channel '''opened'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|He '''opened''' a path through the undergrowth.}}
##: {{ux|en|After we left the forest, the country '''opened''' into a wide, flat plain.}}
##* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=1|passage=I stumbled along through the young pines and huckleberry bushes. Pretty soon I struck into a sort of path that, I cal'lated, might lead to the road I was hunting for. It twisted and turned, and, the first thing I knew, made a sudden bend around a bunch of bayberry scrub and '''opened''' out into a big clear space like a lawn.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To unseal or uncover, or become unsealed or uncovered.
##: {{ux|en|You can '''open''' your eyes now!}}
##: {{ux|en|Her eyes '''opened''' wide.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1996|author=Stephen King|title=Desperation|passage=He had kept on recording everything then, when he had been sure he was going to die, and he went on recording everything now, when he was suddenly consumed with hate for the boy in his arms and overwhelmed by a desire to put something—his motorcycle key would do nicely — into the interfering little prayboy’s throat and '''open''' him like a can of beer.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To [[spread]]; to [[expand]] into an open or [[loose]] position.
##: {{ux|en|to '''open''' a closed fist}}
##: {{ux|en|to '''open''' matted cotton by separating the fibres}}
##: {{ux|en|to '''open''' a map, book, or scroll}}
##: {{ux|en|The flower '''opened''' in the sunlight.}}
##: {{ux|en|Her arms '''opened''' in welcome.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|nursing}} To make (a bed) ready for a patient by folding back the bedcovers.
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2013|author=Susan C. deWit; Patricia A. Williams|title=Fundamental Concepts and Skills for Nursing|page=318
|passage=Follow agency policy, or '''open''' the bed by folding the top linens back.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive|poker}} To reveal one's hand.
##: {{ux|en|Jeff '''opens''' his hand revealing a straight flush.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive|engineering|gas and liquid flow|of valve or damper}} To move to a position allowing fluid to flow.
# To make or become available for use or interaction.
## {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To make or become operative or available.
##: {{ux|en|He '''opened''' a secret bank account in Switzerland.}}
##: {{ux|en|We're '''opening''' the phone lines in ten minutes.}}
##: {{ux|en|Phone lines will '''open''' in ten minutes.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To [[make]] or become [[accessible]] to [[customer]]s, [[client]]s or [[visitor]]s.
##: {{ux|en|I will '''open''' the shop an hour early tomorrow.}}
##: {{ux|en|The summer fete was '''opened''' by the mayor.}}
##: {{ux|en|The shop '''opens''' at 9:00.}}
##: {{ux|en|The fete '''opened''' an hour late because of bad weather.}}
##* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1934
|title=White Unto Harvest in China: A Survey of the Lutheran United Mission, the China Mission of the N.L.C.A., 1890-1934
|url=https://archive.org/details/whiteuntoharvest0000unse/
|OCLC=23011557
|page=76
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/whiteuntoharvest0000unse/page/76/
|text=Suiping was '''opened''' as a main station in 1912 when Rev. H. M. Nesse arrived to take charge of the mission work.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To make receptive or susceptible (to something).
##: {{ux|en|Reading this book will '''open''' you to new ideas.}}
##: {{ux|en|This policy will '''open''' the government to criticism.}}
## {{lb|en|computing|transitive|intransitive}} To connect to a resource (a file, document, etc.) for viewing or editing.
##: {{ux|en|I can't '''open''' the file because someone else is editing it.}}
## {{lb|en|Manglish|Philippines|Quebec}} To [[turn on]]; to [[switch on]].
##: {{ux|en|Please '''open''' the lights, the (electric) fan, the TV.}}
# To start or begin.
## {{lb|en|transitive|or|intransitive}} To start (an event or activity) as the first performer or actor.
##: {{ux|en|A terrible Elvis impersonator '''opened''' the show.}}
##: {{ux|en|Our band '''opened''', and they came on second.}}
##: {{ux|en|Vermont will '''open''' elk hunting season next week.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|with 'for'}} To perform ahead of another at a [[concert]] or [[show]].
##: {{ux|en|Our band '''opened''' for Nirvana.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of an event, activity etc., to [[start]] or get [[underway]].
##: {{ux|en|The meeting '''opened''' with a statement from the chairman.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[bring up]], [[broach]].
##: {{ux|en|I don't want to '''open''' that subject.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[enter]] upon, [[begin]].
##: {{ux|en|to '''open''' a discussion}}
##: {{ux|en|to '''open''' fire upon an enemy}}
##: {{ux|en|to '''open''' trade, or correspondence}}
##: {{ux|en|to '''open''' a case in court, or a meeting}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|cricket}} To [[begin]] a [[side]]'s [[innings]] as one of the first two [[batsman|batsmen]].
## {{lb|en|intransitive|poker}} To [[bet]] before any other player has in a particular betting round in a game of poker.
##: {{ux|en|After the first two players fold, Julie '''opens''' for $5.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive|electricity|of a switch, fuse or circuit breaker}} To move to a position preventing electricity from flowing.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[disclose]]; to [[reveal]]; to [[interpret]]; to [[explain]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1622|author=w:Francis Bacon|title=The History of the Reign of King Henry the Seventh
|passage=The king '''opened''' himself to some of his council, that he was sorry for the earl's death.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|book=Jeremiah|chapter=20|verse=12|passage=Unto thee have I '''opened''' my cause.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Due to the near-opposite meanings relating to fluid flow and electrical components, these usages are deprecated in safety-critical instructions, with the words ''to on'' or ''to off'' preferred, so instead of ''Open valve A; open switch B" use ''Turn valve A to ON; turn switch B to OFF''.

